  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Spotted Helicopter Catfish</li><li>IUCN Red List: Vulnerable (last assessed 2018)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Type locality: Kinabatangan River at Deramakot, Kinabatangan District, Malaysia, Borneo.</p><p>Distribution: Northeast Borneo Pacific, Greater Sunda Island Rivers, Borneo Waters, East Borneo Waters, North Eastern Borneo Waters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Large driftwood as a hiding place is desirable, although not necessary. A bare tank makes cleaning easier.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Will eat only live foods (fish or frogs) at first, but can be trained to take strips of raw meat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>A solitary predator, will attempt to eat any tankmates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Males have a more slender genital papilla immediately in front of the anus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Not known and impossible in the home aquarium, given its size. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Identification: it can be distinguished from W. leerii and W. micropogon by a paler body with a series of distinct dark blotches on the sides of the body below the lateral line.</p><p>Etymology: the specific epithet comes from the Latin maculatus, meaning spot, in reference to the dark blotches on the sides of the body.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1370",
      "html": "<p>Fieldiana Zool. v. 39 (no. 27), p. 279</p>"
    }
